<ComponentPreview name="code-block-demo" />

<PackageInfo>

## Features

- Syntax highlighting for code blocks
- Support for multiple programming languages
- Customizable language selection
- Proper indentation handling
- Markdown code fence shortcuts through `CodeBlockRules.markdown({ on })`
- Typed shortcuts stay literal inside code blocks, and Markdown does not convert pasted plain text

</PackageInfo>

## Kit usage

<Steps>

### Installation

The fastest way to add code blocks is with `CodeBlockKit`. It composes `CodeBlockPlugin` and the removable `CodeHighlightPlugin` with the shipped triple-backtick input rule and [Plate UI](/docs/plate/installation/plate-ui) component.

<ComponentSource name="code-block" />

- [`CodeBlockElement`](/docs/plate/components/code-block): Renders code block containers.

### Add kit

Add the kit to your plugins:

```tsx
import { createEditor } from 'platejs/react';
import { CodeBlockKit } from '@/components/editor/code-block';

const editor = createEditor({
  plugins: [
    // ...otherPlugins,
    ...CodeBlockKit,
  ],
});
```

</Steps>

## High-scale code blocks

`CodeBlockKit` keeps code in native editor DOM. Use
`CodeBlockCodeMirrorElement` explicitly when one code block can contain enough
text or syntax tokens to make that DOM expensive. Plate still owns the
canonical text, selection, history, decorations, and collaboration state;
CodeMirror owns the block's mounted DOM, input surface, and incremental syntax parser.

### Native Plate DOM

This preview contains 1,000 lines. [Open the 10,000-line stress test](/blocks/code-block-huge-demo).

<ComponentPreview name="code-block-huge-demo" id="code-block-preview" />

### CodeMirror

<ComponentPreview name="code-block-codemirror-demo" />

<ComponentSource name="code-block-codemirror" />

For an editor whose code blocks all use CodeMirror, replace the component and omit the Lowlight plugin:

```tsx
import { CodeBlockPlugin, CodeHighlightPlugin } from 'platejs/react';
import {
  CodeBlockCodeMirrorElement,
} from '@/components/editor/code-block-codemirror';
import { createCodeBlockPlugin } from '@/components/editor/code-block';
import { EditorKit } from '@/components/editor/plugins';

const codeBlockCodeMirrorPlugin = createCodeBlockPlugin(
  CodeBlockCodeMirrorElement
);

export const LargeCodeEditorKit = EditorKit
  .filter((plugin) => plugin.name !== CodeHighlightPlugin.name)
  .map((plugin) =>
    plugin.name === CodeBlockPlugin.name ? codeBlockCodeMirrorPlugin : plugin
  );
```

Choose an explicit language from the CodeMirror picker. Parsers load on demand;
plain text and unrecognized language values stay unhighlighted. CodeMirror
keeps neutral range decorations, including overlapping annotations.

For native and CodeMirror views of the same editor, retain `CodeHighlightPlugin`
for the native view. Its syntax decorations carry `data-code-block-syntax`;
CodeMirror excludes those decorations and paints its own syntax. Native views
retain the full code DOM.

<ComponentPreview name="code-block-views-demo" />

This choice is never automatic. Do not switch renderers at a character
threshold, and do not combine a block adapter with whole-document
virtualization.

CodeMirror search (`Mod+F`) finds offscreen code. Browser page Find can inspect
only the mounted viewport DOM. The copy button and print layout use the full
canonical code; printing temporarily renders every line.

### Adapter customization

`createCodeMirrorAdapter` from `platejs/code-block/codemirror` owns the
ExternalText projection. Create it once outside the component and pass it to
`props.slots.externalText` with `config: { language }`.

```tsx
import { EditorView } from '@codemirror/view';
import { createCodeMirrorAdapter } from 'platejs/code-block/codemirror';

const adapter = createCodeMirrorAdapter({
  extensions: [EditorView.lineWrapping],
});
```

The optional entrypoint requires `@codemirror/language`, `@codemirror/state`
and `@codemirror/view`. `extensions` configures presentation, search and native
editing commands. `loadLanguage(language)` returns a CodeMirror extension or
a promise for one; obsolete loads are ignored after configuration changes or
disposal. Omit it for plain text. Keep CodeMirror history extensions disabled:
undo and redo use the editor's shared history.

Plate-authored text, selection, configuration, and decoration transactions
bypass CodeMirror transaction filters so local extensions cannot veto canonical
state. Update listeners observe a local prediction before any synchronous
canonical correction. They must not synchronously dispatch another CodeMirror
transaction or mutate Plate; use a transaction filter to shape the current
transaction, and run later edits from an input handler or command after the
listener returns.

The copied component uses two-space line indentation for Tab and syntax-aware
indentation for Enter. Its language picker, search panel and theme stay in the
component.

## Diagrams

A code block in a diagram language can draw its picture below the source.

<ComponentPreview name="code-block-diagram-demo" />

Add the diagram wrapper and one renderer item for each engine you draw:

```bash
npx shadcn@latest add @plate/code-block-diagram @plate/code-block-diagram-mermaid @plate/code-block-diagram-graphviz
```

<ComponentSource name="code-block-diagram" />

| Item | Renderer | Languages | Packages |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `code-block-diagram-mermaid` | `mermaidRenderer` | `mermaid` | `mermaid` |
| `code-block-diagram-graphviz` | `graphvizRenderer` | `graphviz`, `dot` | `viz.js`, `@types/viz.js` |
| `code-block-diagram-flowchart` | `flowchartRenderer` | `flowchart` | `flowchart.js` |
| `code-block-diagram-plantuml` | `createPlantUmlRenderer({ server })` | `plantuml`, `puml` | `plantuml-encoder`, `@types/plantuml-encoder` |

Wrap the code block component and replace the kit's code-block plugin:

```tsx title="components/editor/diagram-editor-kit.tsx"
import { CodeBlockPlugin } from 'platejs/react';

import {
  CodeBlockElement,
  createCodeBlockPlugin,
} from '@/components/editor/code-block';
import { withDiagrams } from '@/components/editor/code-block-diagram';
import { graphvizRenderer } from '@/components/editor/code-block-diagram-graphviz';
import { mermaidRenderer } from '@/components/editor/code-block-diagram-mermaid';
import { EditorKit } from '@/components/editor/plugins';

const diagramCodeBlockPlugin = createCodeBlockPlugin(
  withDiagrams(CodeBlockElement, {
    renderers: [mermaidRenderer, graphvizRenderer],
  })
);

export const DiagramEditorKit = EditorKit.map((plugin) =>
  plugin.name === CodeBlockPlugin.name ? diagramCodeBlockPlugin : plugin
);
```

`withDiagrams` wraps `CodeBlockCodeMirrorElement` the same way. Each renderer
adds its label to the language picker, and `withDiagrams` throws when two
renderers claim one language. The Mermaid renderer uses your app's Mermaid
configuration and never calls `mermaid.initialize`.

### PlantUML server

PlantUML draws through a server your app runs. The renderer sends each block's
source only to that server, as `<server>/svg/<encoded source>`, and fails
instead of following a redirect.

```tsx
import { createPlantUmlRenderer } from '@/components/editor/code-block-diagram-plantuml';

const plantUmlRenderer = createPlantUmlRenderer({
  server: 'https://plantuml.example.com',
});
```

### Views

A diagram block's toolbar switches between Code, which shows only the source,
and Both, which shows the picture below it. Both is the default in editable and
read-only editors. The view lives in the component; the document stores only the
code block's text and language. Export PNG saves the drawn picture as
`diagram.png`.

A block draws once it is near the viewport and the browser is idle. Each block
runs one render at a time, and only its latest source waits for the next one.

- The picture is an image of the renderer's SVG, with the language as its alt
  text. Mermaid gives most diagram types only a width of 100%, so those pictures
  scale to fit the column, and their PNG takes the browser's default size for
  such an image.
- A failed render shows its error below the source. Output that is not SVG, such
  as Mermaid's iframe under `securityLevel: 'sandbox'`, shows as an error.
- Mermaid builds each drawing in the page under your app's `securityLevel`, so
  with `'loose'` the HTML in a label runs there.
- A language that no renderer claims stays an ordinary code block.
- A diagram block deletes like any other block.

### Insert menu

The shared insert and slash menus have no Diagram entry, because the default
editor kit installs no renderer. An app that draws diagrams adds one to its copy
of the insert menu:

```tsx
import { BaseCodeBlockPlugin } from 'platejs';

const insertItem = {
  icon: <WorkflowIcon />,
  label: 'Diagram',
  value: 'diagram',
  onSelect: () => {
    const codeBlock = editor.plugin(BaseCodeBlockPlugin);

    if (!codeBlock.installed || editor.read.view.isReadOnly()) return;

    codeBlock.update.insert({ language: 'mermaid' }, { select: true });
  },
};
```

### Static and export output

Static rendering and Markdown export draw no picture. A diagram block keeps its
source as an ordinary code block, and Markdown writes it as a code fence with
its language, such as `mermaid`.

## Manual usage

<Steps>

### Installation

```bash
npm install platejs lowlight
```

### Add plugins

Add `CodeBlockPlugin` for code-block editing. Add `CodeHighlightPlugin` separately when you want Lowlight syntax highlighting.

```tsx
import { CodeBlockPlugin } from 'platejs/react';
import { createEditor } from 'platejs/react';

const editor = createEditor({
  plugins: [
    // ...otherPlugins,
    CodeBlockPlugin,
  ],
});
```

### Configure plugins

Configure each capability where it is owned. `CodeBlockPlugin` owns the multiline text and editing behavior. `CodeHighlightPlugin` owns highlighting.

**Basic Setup with All Languages:**

```tsx
import { CodeBlockRules } from 'platejs';
import { CodeBlockPlugin, CodeHighlightPlugin } from 'platejs/react';
import { createEditor } from 'platejs/react';
import { CodeBlockElement } from '@/components/editor/code-block';
import { createCodeBlockLowlight } from '@/components/editor/code-block-lowlight';

const lowlight = createCodeBlockLowlight();

const editor = createEditor({
  plugins: [
    // ...otherPlugins,
    CodeBlockPlugin.configure({
      component: CodeBlockElement,
      inputRules: [CodeBlockRules.markdown({ on: 'match' })],
      shortcuts: { toggle: { keys: 'mod+alt+8' } },
    }),
    CodeHighlightPlugin.configure({
      initialState: { lowlight },
    }),
  ],
});
```

**Custom Language Setup (Optimized Bundle):**

For optimized bundle size, you can register only specific languages:

```tsx
import { createLowlight } from 'lowlight';
import css from 'highlight.js/lib/languages/css';
import js from 'highlight.js/lib/languages/javascript';
import ts from 'highlight.js/lib/languages/typescript';
import html from 'highlight.js/lib/languages/xml';

// Create a lowlight instance
const lowlight = createLowlight();

// Register only the languages you need
lowlight.register('html', html);
lowlight.register('css', css);
lowlight.register('js', js);
lowlight.register('ts', ts);

const editor = createEditor({
  plugins: [
    // ...otherPlugins,
    CodeBlockPlugin.configure({
      component: CodeBlockElement,
      inputRules: [CodeBlockRules.markdown({ on: 'match' })],
      shortcuts: { toggle: { keys: 'mod+alt+8' } },
    }),
    CodeHighlightPlugin.configure({
      initialState: {
        lowlight,
        defaultLanguage: 'js', // Set default language (optional)
      },
    }),
  ],
});
```

- `CodeBlockPlugin.configure({ component })`: Assigns
  [`CodeBlockElement`](/docs/plate/components/code-block) to render code block
  containers.
- `inputRules`: Registers the triple-backtick fence rule. Use `on: 'match'` to commit when the fence becomes complete or `on: 'break'` to commit on `Enter`.
- `CodeHighlightPlugin.initialState.lowlight`: Lowlight instance for syntax highlighting. The plugin reads the supplied registry without modifying it. The copied `createCodeBlockLowlight()` resource registers the shipped browser-safe Python grammar and its `py`, `gyp`, and `ipython` aliases before the editor uses it.
- `CodeHighlightPlugin.initialState.defaultLanguage`: Default language when no language is specified.
- `shortcuts.toggle`: Defines a keyboard [shortcut](/docs/plate/plugin-shortcuts) to toggle code blocks.

Each `codeBlock` contains one text child. Newline characters define physical lines; editing and highlighting derive their line behavior from text offsets.

Inside a code block no [input rule](/docs/plate/plugin-input-rules#where-rules-run) runs, so `# `, `**x**`, `->` and URLs stay as typed. Neither Markdown nor an input rule converts pasted plain text.

For plain code blocks, omit `CodeHighlightPlugin`:

```tsx
import { CodeBlockPlugin } from 'platejs/react';
import { createEditor } from 'platejs/react';

const editor = createEditor({
  plugins: [CodeBlockPlugin],
});
```

For the runtime model, see [Plugin Input Rules](/docs/plate/plugin-input-rules).

### Turn into toolbar button

Add a structural action that calls `BaseCodeBlockPlugin` directly. Enable it only for a contiguous sibling selection with one homogeneous active state.

```tsx
import { BaseCodeBlockPlugin, PLUGINS } from 'platejs';

const turnIntoItem = {
  icon: <FileCodeIcon />,
  label: 'Code',
  value: PLUGINS.codeBlock,
  onSelect: () => {
    const codeBlock = editor.plugin(BaseCodeBlockPlugin);

    if (!codeBlock.installed || editor.read.view.isReadOnly()) return;

    codeBlock.update.toggle();
  },
};
```

### Insert toolbar button

Add an item whose callback calls the installed code-block feature:

```tsx
import { BaseCodeBlockPlugin, PLUGINS } from 'platejs';

const insertItem = {
  icon: <FileCodeIcon />,
  label: 'Code',
  value: PLUGINS.codeBlock,
  onSelect: () => {
    const codeBlock = editor.plugin(BaseCodeBlockPlugin);

    if (!codeBlock.installed || editor.read.view.isReadOnly()) return;

    codeBlock.update.insert({}, { select: true });
  },
};
```

</Steps>

## Plugins

### `CodeBlockPlugin`

Owns the code-block schema, multiline editing behavior, input rules, shortcuts, and commands.

### `CodeHighlightPlugin`

Produces transient syntax decorations with Lowlight. Syntax tokens are not stored as text marks. Include or omit this descriptor as one ordinary plugin-array entry.

<API name="CodeHighlightPlugin">
<APIOptions>
  <APIItem name="defaultLanguage" type="string | null" optional>
    Default language to use when no language is specified. Set to null to disable syntax highlighting by default.
  </APIItem>
  <APIItem name="lowlight" type="ReturnType<typeof createLowlight> | null" optional>
    Lowlight instance to use for highlighting. If not provided, syntax highlighting will be disabled.
  </APIItem>
</APIOptions>
</API>

## Commands

Install `CodeBlockPlugin`, then mutate code blocks through its command group.

```tsx
editor.update.codeBlock.insert();
editor.update.codeBlock.toggle();
editor.update.codeBlock.tab();
editor.update.codeBlock.untab();
editor.update.codeBlock.resetBlock();
editor.update.codeBlock.selectAll();
```

- `insert()` converts an empty selected block in place. For non-empty or
  expanded selections, it inserts and selects a paragraph after the range-end
  block, then converts that paragraph. It is a no-op without a selection.
- `toggle()` converts selected blocks to or from code blocks and returns
  whether it changed the document. On a Details title it converts the title
  alone and leaves the body after the code block. It returns `false` when the
  selection holds the title and more blocks, and when the selection is in a
  named root that the editor or view running it does not edit.
- `tab()` and `untab()` indent or outdent selected physical lines by two spaces.
- `resetBlock()` unwraps the selected code block into paragraphs.
- `selectAll()` selects the containing code block.

## Queries

Read code-block state through the installed descriptor:

```tsx
const codeBlock = editor.plugin(CodeBlockPlugin);

codeBlock.read.entry();
codeBlock.read.isEmpty();
codeBlock.read.indentDepth();
```

- `entry({ at? })` returns `{ codeBlock: [element, path] }` for the containing code block, or `undefined`. It uses the current selection when `at` is omitted.
- `isEmpty()` returns whether the containing code block has no text. It returns `false` outside a code block.
- `indentDepth()` returns the number of leading whitespace characters on the anchor's physical line, or `0` outside a code block.
